/*! \mainpage COMP 345 Intermediate Project (Team VRW) - Tower Defense: analysis, design and tools

\section overview Overview

The project is a tower defense game made of three parts that work together, plus a thin native Windows graphical driver:

| Part | Folder | Classes | What it does |
|------|--------|---------|--------------|
| 1. Map | `Map/` | Map, CellType | Grid of path and scenery cells with one entry and one exit, and a validity check. |
| 2. Towers | `Towers/` | Tower, BasicTower, SplashTower, SlowTower | Towers that are bought, upgraded, sold and shoot critters. |
| 3. Critters | `Critters/` | Critter, CritterGroup, CritterGroupGenerator | Waves of critters that walk the path, die for a reward or leak through the exit, steal coins and cost a life. |
| 4. Graphical driver | `Gui/` | WinGui.cpp | Win32 editor and game view that draws and forwards input without changing the rules. |
| Observers | `Observer.h`, `Towers/`, `Map/`, `Critters/` | Observer, Observable, TowerObserver, MapObserver, CritterObserver, CritterGroupObserver | Observer pattern: Tower, Map, Critter and CritterGroup are observable, and each view is re-displayed every time its subject changes (see \ref design_observer). |

| Assignment 3 patterns | `Towers/`, `Critters/` | TargetingStrategy (+4 strategies), CritterFactory, StandardCritterFactory, TowerDecorator (+4 decorators) | Strategy: swappable tower targeting. Factory: wave creation. Decorator: tower upgrades added during play (see \ref design_strategy, \ref design_factory, \ref design_decorator). |
Every part has its own driver and test program, and `GameDriver.cpp` plays several waves with the three parts together.
Build and run instructions are in `README.md`.

\section rules Analysis: game rules

\subsection rules_source Where the rules come from

 - **Bloons TD** gives the overall rules: the enemies follow a fixed track from an entry to an exit, towers are placed beside the
   track, every round is harder than the previous one, the player earns cash for every enemy popped and loses lives when an enemy
   leaks through the exit, and the enemies of later rounds come in a tighter stream.
   See the Bloons Wiki pages <a href="https://bloons.fandom.com/wiki/Rounds_(BTD6)">Rounds</a> and
   <a href="https://bloons.fandom.com/wiki/Lives">Lives</a>. Here the player has 10 lives and loses one for every critter that leaks; the game ends at 0 lives, as the
   project description says ("the game ends when a certain number of critters are able to reach the end point").
 - **League of Legends** gives the critters: the lane minions (melee, caster, cannon and super minion) that leave their base one
   after the other in waves, with stats and gold bounty that grow as the game goes on.
   See <a href="https://wiki.leagueoflegends.com/en-us/Minion_(League_of_Legends)">League of Legends Wiki: Minion</a>.
 - The rest comes straight from the project description.

\subsection rules_list Rules implemented

Map
 -# The map is a grid of any size chosen when it is created. A cell is either PATH or SCENERY.
 -# Critters walk only on PATH cells, towers stand only on SCENERY cells.
 -# There is exactly one entry cell and one exit cell, both PATH cells and different from each other.
 -# The entry and the exit are connected by PATH cells (moving up, down, left or right). Otherwise the map is invalid.
 -# A map can be saved to a text file and loaded again (`saveToFile`, `loadFromFile`), so the player can choose a saved map to play on.

Towers
 -# A tower has a buying cost, a refund value, a range, a power (damage) and a rate of fire.
 -# There are three types that differ in what a shot does: direct damage, area damage and damage plus slowing.
 -# A tower has levels (1 to 3). Each level is more powerful and has to be paid for. A tower can be sold at any time for a
    refund that depends on the coins invested in it.
 -# A working tower detects the critters in its range, selects one and shoots it.

Critters
 -# A critter has a reward, hit points, strength, speed and level.
 -# A critter group is made at the start of every wave, every wave is harder than the previous one.
 -# The critters of a wave enter the map one after the other through the entry.
 -# A critter knows where the exit is, decides where to move and moves at its speed.
 -# Every cell holds only one game element: two critters are never on the same path cell (see Movement in the design).
 -# A critter that reaches zero hit points dies and the player gets coins proportional to its level.
 -# A critter that reaches the exit steals coins from the player according to its strength.
 -# A critter that reaches the exit also costs the player one life. The player starts with 10 lives and the game is over
    (GAME OVER screen, then restart or go back to the editor) when no lives are left.

Assignment 3 (design patterns)
 -# **Targeting.** A tower first keeps the critters that are alive and inside its range, then chooses one of them with its
    targeting option. The four options are the ones of Bloons TD (see
    <a href="https://www.bloonswiki.com/Targeting_option">Blooncyclopedia: Targeting option</a>):
    *Close* = nearest critter to the tower (the default), *First* = nearest critter to the exit (the one that walked the most path
    cells), *Strong* = most hit points left, *Weak* = fewest hit points left. On a tie the first critter found is kept.
    The option can be changed at any time during play.
 -# **Waves.** Every wave is made by a wave factory from the wave number N (waves destroyed so far + 1): 3 melee and 3 caster
    critters, N/3 cannon critters and N/10 super critters, all of level N, released every 0.8 s x max(1 - 0.04 (N - 1), 0.4).
    Later waves are larger, stronger and denser, as in the Bloons TD rounds and the League of Legends minion waves cited above.
 -# **Upgrades during play.** A tower is upgraded by adding upgrades to it while the game runs, and they can be combined:
    - *Level up*: the normal way to go up a level (1 to 3). It costs `cost x current level` (100 then 200 for a basic tower),
      gives damage and range x1.2 (two level-ups give x1.44) and fire rate x1.1, and half of its price is added to the refund.
    - *Splash*: the critters within 1.5 cells of the target also take half of the tower's damage.
    - *Burning*: the target keeps taking 20% of the hit 3 more times, once per second. A new hit restarts the burn, a critter
      never burns twice at the same time.
    - *Freezing*: the target is slowed (half speed for 2 s by default). A new hit restarts the slow, it does not add up.
    Splash (150 coins), Burning (125) and Freezing (100) can each be bought once per tower, at any level. Their price is not
    refunded when the tower is sold. In the GUI the player buys every upgrade from the selected-tower panel. In the integrated
    console game (`GameDriver.cpp`) the towers level up between waves, and a tower at its maximum level buys one special upgrade:
    the basic tower gets Burning, the splash tower Freezing, the slow tower Splash.

\section design Design

\subsection design_overview Structure

\verbatim
   Map  <----------- reads ------------  Critter  <---- owned by ---- CritterGroup <---- creates ---- CritterGroupGenerator
    ^                                       ^                              |
    | isSceneryCell (placement rule)        | getRow, getColumn, isAlive   | getActiveCritters()
    |                                       | takeDamage, applySlow        v
  GameDriver  ------------------------>  Tower (BasicTower, SplashTower, SlowTower)  <--- update(deltaTime, critters)
\endverbatim

The three parts only know each other through small interfaces: towers use five functions of Critter, critters use five read-only
functions of Map (`getRows`, `getColumns`, `getEntry`, `getExit`, `isPathCell`) and neither of them knows about the game loop.
Coins are always passed as `int&` (`Tower::purchase`, `upgrade`, `sell` and `CritterGroup::update`), so the player's balance
lives in one variable owned by the game.

\subsection design_loop One game tick

The game loop (see `playWave` in `GameDriver.cpp`) repeats these steps with a small time step (0.1 s):
 -# every tower gets `update(deltaTime, group.getActiveCritters())`: cooldown goes down, a target is selected and shot if possible;
 -# `group.update(deltaTime, map, coins)`: spawns the critters whose time has come, moves every critter, adds the reward of the
    critters killed in step 1 and subtracts the coins stolen by the critters that reached the exit;
 -# the wave is over when `group.isFinished()` is true. Between waves the player buys, upgrades or sells towers.

Towers shoot first so a critter that is killed is paid in the same tick and is never moved afterwards.

\subsection design_map Part 1: Map

 - The grid is a `vector<vector<CellType>>` that starts as all SCENERY, so a blank map of any size is created with
   `Map(rows, columns)` (a size that is not positive throws `std::invalid_argument`).
 - `setCell` changes a cell, `setEntry` and `setExit` store the coordinates and turn that cell into PATH. All of them return `false`
   for coordinates outside the map. The entry and the exit cannot be the same cell and cannot be turned into SCENERY.
 - `isValidMap` checks that entry and exit are set, inside the map, different, PATH cells, and connected.
 - `hasConnectedPath` is a breadth-first search from the entry over PATH cells with four-direction moves.
 - Read-only accessors (`getRows`, `getColumns`, `getEntry`, `getExit`, `isPathCell`, `isSceneryCell`) let the other parts use the map
   without being able to change it. `isSceneryCell` is what a game uses to refuse a tower placed on the path.
 - Choice: the map does not store critters or Tower objects. For the map observer it keeps one letter per cell for the towers
   standing on it (`placeTower`, `moveTower`, `removeTower`), so a tower can be shown and moved on the map without the Map
   knowing the Tower class. A tower only stands on free SCENERY, and a cell with a tower cannot become path, entry or exit.

\subsection design_towers Part 2: Towers

`Tower` is an abstract base class. The shared behavior (buying, upgrading, selling, range, cooldown, target selection) lives in
the base class and each type only provides its own `applyAttack` and `getName`. This is the template method pattern:
`Tower::update` is always the same and calls the virtual `applyAttack` for the part that changes.

| Type | Cost | Damage | Range | Shots/s | Special |
|------|-----:|-------:|------:|--------:|---------|
| BasicTower  | 100 | 20 | 3.0 | 1.0 | direct damage to one critter |
| SplashTower | 150 | 30 | 2.0 | 0.5 | damages every critter within 1.5 cells of the target |
| SlowTower   | 125 | 5  | 3.0 | 1.0 | small damage and halves the target's speed for 2 s |

 - **Levels:** maximum level 3. Going up one level costs `cost x current level`. In Assignment 1 this was `Tower::upgrade`
   (+25% damage rounded up, +0.5 range, +10% fire rate); it is kept for the Assignment 1 and observer tests and drivers, but the
   game and the GUI now level towers up with the Decorator pattern (see \ref design_decorator).
 - **Refund:** half of all the coins invested (purchase plus upgrades), available at any time with `getRefundValue`.
 - **Targeting:** among the alive critters inside the range, the one closest to the tower is selected.
 - **Cooldown:** after a shot the tower waits `1 / fireRate` seconds. A tower that is not bought, or already sold, never shoots.
 - Choice: the target is chosen by distance because it is simple to explain and test. More rules (first, strongest) would fit in `selectTarget`.

\subsection design_critters Part 3: Critters

Three small classes with one job each.

| Class | Responsibility |
|-------|----------------|
| Critter | One critter: stats, shortest path, movement, damage, slow, and one-time `claimReward` / `claimPenalty`. |
| CritterGroup | One wave: releases the critters one after the other, updates them, settles the coins. |
| CritterGroupGenerator | Called at the start of a wave, `generate(wave, map)` builds the group. Refuses wave numbers below 1 and invalid maps. |

**Critter types** (stats at level 1, taken from the idea of the lane minions of League of Legends):

| Type | Hit points | Strength | Speed (cells/s) | Reward | Critters in a wave |
|------|-----------:|---------:|----------------:|-------:|--------------------|
| Melee  | 30  | 1 | 1.0 | 2  | 3 in every wave |
| Caster | 20  | 2 | 1.1 | 3  | 3 in every wave |
| Cannon | 60  | 3 | 0.8 | 5  | wave / 3 (first one in wave 3) |
| Super  | 120 | 5 | 0.9 | 10 | wave / 10 (first one in wave 10) |

The numbers are our own choices. In the game the cannon minion only appears in some waves, here the extra critters stay in the
following waves so that no wave is easier than the one before.

**Difficulty:** the level of every critter is the wave number, and from level 1 to level n:
 - hit points grow 20% of the base value per level;
 - strength grows by 1 every 3 levels;
 - speed grows 3% per level, at most +50%;
 - the reward is `base reward x level` (proportional to the level, as the project description asks);
 - the time between two critters is 0.8 s in wave 1 and 4% shorter every wave (never below 0.32 s), like the tighter streams of
   later Bloons TD rounds.
Because stats only go up and a wave never loses critters, the total hit points of a wave strictly increase (checked for waves 1 to 30).

**Movement:** when a critter spawns it runs a breadth-first search from the entry to the exit (it knows the exit from the map) and keeps
the shortest path. Each update adds `speed x deltaTime` to its progress and moves it to the next cell of the path every time the
progress reaches 1, so the speed does not depend on the size of the time step. A critter that reaches the last cell escapes.

**One critter per cell:** the project description says a cell holds only one game element. `CritterGroup::update` gives every critter a function
that tells whether a cell is taken by another active critter. Before a critter steps onto its next cell it asks that function; if the
cell is taken it stays at 0.8 of its current cell (right behind the critter in front) and tries again on the next update. Critters
never pass each other, so the critters are updated front first: the one in front moves and frees its cell for the one behind.
A new critter is released only when the entry is free, so a critter that is due can be delayed a few ticks behind a slower one. The exit is never
blocked, because a critter that reaches it leaves the map. A single critter used without a group has no neighbours and walks freely.

**Damage, reward and penalty:**
 - `takeDamage` lowers the hit points, never below zero, and kills the critter at zero. Only a critter that is on the map can be hit.
 - `claimReward` returns the reward of a dead critter once, `claimPenalty` returns the strength of an escaped critter once.
   `CritterGroup::update` calls them every tick, so rewards and penalties can never be counted twice.
 - A critter that escapes steals as many coins as its strength, and the player never goes below 0 coins.
 - A slow (`applySlow`) multiplies the speed by a factor for some seconds. A stronger slow replaces a weaker one.

\subsection design_observer Observer pattern (Tower, Map and Critter observers)

\verbatim
          Observer (interface)                      Observable (subject)
      + update(Observable*) = 0          1   *   - observers : vector<Observer*>
      + subjectDestroyed(Observable*)  <-------- + attach(Observer*) / detach(Observer*)
                 ^                                # notify()   -> calls update(this) on every observer
                 |                                           ^
   +-------------+--------------+------------------+         |
   |             |              |                  |         +----- Tower      (Part 1)
TowerObserver MapObserver CritterObserver CritterGroupObserver +--- Map        (Part 2)
   |  observes   |  observes    |  observes        | observes  +--- Critter    (Part 3)
   v             v              v                  v           +--- CritterGroup (Part 3, add / remove)
 Tower          Map           Critter          CritterGroup
\endverbatim

 - `Observer.h` holds the two base classes (header only, so every existing build command still works). `Observable` keeps the
   list of observers; `notify()` is protected, so only the observed class itself decides when it has changed.
 - **Pull model:** `update(subject)` only says "I changed"; the view reads the new values from the subject through its getters.
   The subjects never know how they are displayed, and the views never change the subjects.
 - **Notify only on real changes.** A refused action (upgrade past level 3, tower moved onto the path, removing a critter that
   does not exist, hitting a dead critter) and an action that changes nothing (a cell set to the type it already has) do not notify.
   A critter notifies when it enters a new cell, not on every small step inside a cell, so the view is not redrawn every tick.

| Subject | Changes that call `notify()` | View |
|---------|------------------------------|------|
| Tower | `purchase`, `upgrade`, `sell`, `moveTo`, a shot in `update` (cooldown reset) | `TowerObserver`: type, cell, status, level, damage, range, fire rate, cooldown, costs, refund, splash radius or slow |
| Map | `setCell`, `setEntry`, `setExit`, `placeTower`, `moveTower`, `removeTower`, `loadFromFile` (once) | `MapObserver`: the grid with coordinates, towers, number of towers and whether the map is valid |
| Critter | `spawn`, entering a new cell, slow wearing off, escaping (all in `update`), `takeDamage`, `applySlow` | `CritterObserver`: type, level, state, cell and step on the path, hit point bar, speed (and slow), strength, reward |
| CritterGroup | `addCritter`, `removeCritter`, an `update` in which a critter spawns, changes cell, is hit, dies or escapes | `CritterGroupObserver`: one line per critter of the wave, total hit points, spawned, killed, escaped, coins |

**Lifetime and copies** (the classes are copied by value in the existing code: `CritterGroup` holds a `vector<Critter>`, the GUI
assigns `state.map = Map(...)`, `loadFromFile` does `*this = loaded`):
 - a copy of an observable starts with no observers, because an observer watches one object, not its values;
 - an assignment keeps the object's own observers, so a loaded map is still displayed by the same view (`loadFromFile` then notifies once);
 - each view attaches itself in its constructor and detaches in its destructor; if the subject is destroyed first, it calls
   `subjectDestroyed` and the view forgets the pointer, so there is never a dangling pointer;
 - `notify` iterates over a copy of the list, so an observer may detach itself while it is being notified.

**Part 3 (add / remove):** the project's Critter is one critter, and the critters of a wave live in `CritterGroup`. So both are
observable: `CritterObserver` shows one critter changing, and `CritterGroupObserver` shows critters being added to and removed from
the wave (`addCritter` adds a WAITING critter at the end of the wave, `removeCritter(index)` also works on a critter that already
spawned). As with any vector, adding or removing critters invalidates the pointers returned by `getActiveCritters`.

**Display:** the views draw text in the console (or into any `std::ostream`, which the tests use to read what was drawn). The
assignment lets us choose how the view is displayed; text keeps the drivers portable and testable. Because the views only use the
observer interface, a graphical view (for example a Win32 panel in `Gui/WinGui.cpp`) can be attached the same way without changing
Tower, Map or Critter.

**Drivers** (`make observers`):
 - `tower_observer_driver`: creates a Slow tower, plugs a TowerObserver, then buys, upgrades twice, tries a refused upgrade, moves,
   shoots a critter and sells it. Every view after the first one is drawn by the observer pattern.
 - `map_observer_driver`: builds a 7 x 7 map, plugs a MapObserver, then places two towers, moves them (one refused move onto the path),
   adds a shortcut, moves the exit (the view shows the map becoming invalid), reconnects it and removes a tower.
 - `critter_observer_driver`: plugs a CritterObserver to a critter that spawns, walks, is hit, slowed and killed; then plugs a
   CritterGroupObserver to the wave 2 group and adds and removes critters (including a refused removal and a critter already on the map).

\subsection design_strategy Strategy pattern: tower targeting (Assignment 3, Part 1)

| Role | Class |
|------|-------|
| Strategy interface | `TargetingStrategy` (one pure virtual method: `selectTarget(candidates)`) |
| Concrete strategies | `NearestToTowerStrategy` (default), `NearestToExitStrategy`, `StrongestCritterStrategy`, `WeakestCritterStrategy` |
| Context | `Tower`, which owns a `std::unique_ptr<TargetingStrategy>` |

 - `Tower::selectTarget` only builds the list of candidates (non-null, alive, in range) and hands it to the strategy, so the
   choice of target exists in exactly one place: the strategy. The old hard-coded "closest critter" loop was moved into
   `NearestToTowerStrategy`, so the default behaviour of every tower is unchanged.
 - `Tower::setTargetingStrategy` swaps the strategy at run time and calls `notify()` (the Observer views refresh).
   Passing `nullptr` restores the default.
 - The strategies only see critters, never the tower's internals. `NearestToTowerStrategy` keeps a pointer to its tower and
   reads `getRow()`/`getColumn()` on every call, so it follows the tower when it is moved.
 - The GUI preview of the next shot (`predictTarget`) now asks `tower.selectTarget`, so it always matches the tower's strategy.

\subsection design_factory Factory pattern: critter waves (Assignment 3, Part 2)

| Role | Class |
|------|-------|
| Creator interface | `CritterFactory` (`createGroup(waveNumber, map)`, returns a `CritterGroup`) |
| Concrete creator | `StandardCritterFactory` |
| Product | `CritterGroup` (the critters of one wave) |

 - The game (`GameDriver.cpp`, `Gui/WinGui.cpp`) only asks a `CritterFactory` for wave N. How many critters of which type and
   level, and how fast they are released, is the factory's business. Another difficulty curve (an easy mode, an endless mode)
   would be a new subclass, without touching the game loop.
 - `StandardCritterFactory` holds a `CritterGroupGenerator` and delegates to it, so the composition rules written for
   Assignment 1 stay in one place; the factory adds the common interface. `critter_factory_tests` checks that both produce
   identical waves for waves 1 to 30.
 - Wave numbers below 1 and invalid maps are refused with `std::invalid_argument`.

\subsection design_decorator Decorator pattern: tower upgrades (Assignment 3, Part 3)

| Role | Class |
|------|-------|
| Component | `Tower` (public operations made `virtual`) |
| Concrete components | `BasicTower`, `SplashTower`, `SlowTower` |
| Decorator | `TowerDecorator`: is a `Tower`, owns the wrapped `Tower*` and forwards every call to it |
| Concrete decorators | `LevelUpDecorator`, `SplashDecorator`, `BurningDecorator`, `FreezingDecorator` |

 - A decorated tower is still a `Tower`, so it goes in the same `std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Tower>>` as the others and the game
   code does not change: `tower.reset(new BurningDecorator(tower.release()));`. Decorators stack in any order and number.
 - **How a shot goes through the chain.** The innermost tower keeps the state (cooldown, purchased, level). When the outermost
   decorator is updated, it calls the protected `performUpdate(deltaTime, critters, attacker)` down the chain with itself as the
   attacker. The innermost tower checks its cooldown, selects the target (with its strategy, but using the attacker's range, so a
   Level up also widens the targeting) and calls `attacker.applyAttack(...)`. Each decorator's `applyAttack` first runs the
   wrapped attack, then adds its own effect. `TowerDecorator` is a friend of `Tower` only so it can forward these protected calls.
 - **Level up is the upgrade.** Each level bought wraps the tower in one more `LevelUpDecorator`, which overrides:
   `getLevel` (wrapped level + 1, so the "Lv2" in the name and the level always agree), `getDamage`/`getRange` (x1.2),
   `getFireRate` (x1.1, and the cooldown uses the attacker's fire rate so it really shoots faster) and `getRefundValue`
   (wrapped refund + half of the price paid, which the decorator stores). On each hit it adds the extra damage of its level to the
   target. `TowerDecorator::getUpgradeCost` computes the next price from the decorated level (`getCost() x getLevel()`, 0 at the
   maximum level), and `TowerDecorator::sell` pays the decorated refund. `LevelUpDecorator::upgrade` always returns false, so the
   Assignment 1 `Tower::upgrade` can never run under it and count a level twice.
 - **Buying upgrades.** `TowerUpgrades.h` holds the helpers used by the game and the GUI: `levelUp(tower, coins)` checks that the
   tower is bought, not sold, below its maximum level and affordable, takes the coins, then does
   `tower.reset(new LevelUpDecorator(tower.release(), cost))`. `addSplash`, `addBurning` and `addFreezing` do the same with a fixed
   price, once per tower. `baseTower` and `findDecorator<D>` walk the chain (the GUI uses them for the tower's color and effects).
 - The targeting strategy lives in the innermost tower, and `getTargetingStrategy` is forwarded, so a strategy chosen before an
   upgrade is kept after it.
 - `BurningDecorator` keeps one burn per critter (damage per tick, ticks left, time since the last tick) and advances the burns
   every time the chain is updated, for the critters in the list passed to the update only, so a critter that left the game
   (dead, escaped, or a finished wave) is never touched through a stale pointer.
 - Names describe the chain: `Basic Tower Lv2 +Splash +Freezing +Burning`.
 - Mutating calls on a decorator (`purchase`, `upgrade`, `sell`, `moveTo`, `update`, `setTargetingStrategy`) call `notify()` on
   the decorator, so observers attached to it stay up to date.
 - Ownership: the decorator deletes the tower it wraps, so deleting the outermost object frees the whole chain
   (checked in `tower_decorator_tests`, and the test programs run clean under AddressSanitizer).
 - Towers are no longer copyable (they own their strategy); the game already stored them in `std::unique_ptr`.

\subsection design_choices Other design choices

 - No inheritance for critters: the types only differ in numbers, so one class with a `CritterType` is enough.
 - Time is passed in seconds to every `update`, so a console loop, or a GUI later, decides how often things are updated.
 - Critters keep their own copy of the path instead of a pointer to the map, so they cannot be left pointing at a deleted map.
 - Tests and drivers use only the public interface of each class.

\section tools Tools and libraries

| Choice | Why |
|--------|-----|
| C++17 with g++ | Required language, g++ is available in the labs, and C++17 gives `inline` variables and `make_unique`. |
| Standard library only (`vector`, `queue`, `memory`, `cmath`, `algorithm`, `iostream`) for the other drivers | Nothing to install for the console build, so the marker can compile it anywhere. |
| Win32/GDI for the graphical driver | No extra GUI library is needed on Windows, the API ships with the MinGW toolchain, the only GUI code lives in `Gui/WinGui.cpp`, the game classes do not know Win32, and `make gui` is separate so the rest builds without it. |
| Console output for the other drivers | Simple and portable. The map is drawn with characters (E entry, X exit, . path, # scenery, B/P/L towers, M/C/N/S critters). |
| Makefile plus plain g++ commands | One command builds everything, and the commands are also listed for computers without make. |
| Own `check` helper in `TestHelper.h` | A testing framework would be one more thing to install, and the tests are small. |
| Doxygen | The documentation is made from the `///` comments and this page. |

\section testing Testing

Each part has a test program that prints PASS or FAIL for every check and returns a non-zero exit code if one fails (`make test`).

| Program | Checks | What it covers |
|---------|-------:|----------------|
| `map_tests` | 28 | Blank maps and sizes, setting cells, entry and exit rules, valid and invalid maps (no exit, gap in the path, diagonal-only link), saving a map to a file and loading it back, a missing file keeps the old map. |
| `tower_tests` | 24 | Buying, upgrading to the maximum level, refund, selling, range, closest target, cooldown, splash radius, slowing, kills. |
| `observer_tests` | 63 | Observable base (attach twice, detach, copies, assignment, self-detach, subject destroyed), and for each of Tower, Map, Critter and CritterGroup: every change re-displays the view, refused or unchanged actions do not, what the view shows, tower placement rules on the map, adding and removing critters. |
| `critter_tests` | 60 | Stats and reward proportional to the level, damage and death, movement and shortest path with a dead end, slow, wave composition and difficulty from wave 1 to 30, sequential spawning, one critter per cell (waiting behind a blocked cell, queueing behind a slower critter, no spawn on an occupied entry), rewards paid once, coins stolen, coins never below 0. |
| `tower_strategy_tests` | 25 | Each strategy on a known list (distinct answer for each), empty list, stable tie-breaks, default strategy, swapping and resetting with `nullptr`, filtering of null, dead and out-of-range critters, notification on change, `update` shooting the chosen critter. |
| `critter_factory_tests` | 19 | Wave 1 = 3 melee + 3 caster, cannon from wave 3, super from wave 10, size formula and monotonic size for waves 1 to 30, levels = wave number, spawn interval formula, same waves as `CritterGroupGenerator`, invalid wave numbers and invalid map. |
| `tower_decorator_tests` | 76 | Level up once and twice (stats, names), forwarding of every operation and notifications, ownership, range of a decorated tower, names with all labels, splash (including when the target dies), burning ticks, restart and cleanup, freezing and clamping, a fully stacked tower in a real shot, strategies through decorators. `levelUp`: level and name go up, next cost rises and is 0 at the maximum level, refund rises, refused without coins, at the maximum level, on a tower not bought, sold or null; `upgrade()` refused under a level-up decorator. Special upgrade helpers: price taken once, no duplicates, refused without coins. The targeting strategy is kept through upgrades. |

The drivers show the same behavior on screen: `map_driver`, `tower_driver` (buy, upgrade, sell and shoot), `critter_driver`
(generated waves, critters walking, rewards and stolen coins) and `game_driver` (all parts together over 10 waves).
The Assignment 3 drivers are `tower_strategy_driver` (one tower, the same four critters, the four strategies in turn),
`critter_factory_driver` (waves 1, 5 and 10 and the difficulty curve) and `tower_decorator_driver` (each decorator alone, stacked, levels bought with `levelUp()` showing the next cost and the refund,
then a fully upgraded tower shooting critters step by step); `make patterns` runs the three of them. `game_driver` uses the three
patterns in a real game.
\section gui Graphical driver

The Win32 graphical driver is a thin layer on top of the same rules used by the console drivers. It does not change the game logic.

 - **Edit mode:** choose Path, Scenery, Entry or Exit, then click or drag on the grid. Rows and columns are chosen separately and can be resized between 3 and 40, so rectangular maps such as 5 x 30 or 10 x 10 are possible. The "Size step" button makes Rows and Cols change by 1, 5 or 10 at a time. Resizing keeps the path cells, the entry and the exit that still fit. The cell size is computed from the map size (the biggest square cell that fits the map area), and the labels, tower letters and level dots are only drawn when they fit in the cell. Blank map and Sample map are shortcuts for the current size or for the 7x7 map used in `GameDriver.cpp`.
 - **Play mode:** buy towers on scenery cells, select a tower to see its stats and buttons, start waves, pause or resume the fixed-step simulation, and switch back to the editor when no wave is running.
 - **Selected tower (Assignment 3):** clicking a tower replaces the shop with its controls (click an empty cell to get the shop back):
   - *Level up* (price, or MAX): Decorator pattern, calls `levelUp`.
   - *Target: Nearest / Exit / Strongest / Weakest*: Strategy pattern, each click switches to the next strategy with
     `setTargetingStrategy`. The current strategy is read back with `getTargetingStrategy`, and the shot preview follows it.
   - *+Splash*, *+Burning*, *+Freezing* (price, or Owned): Decorator pattern, disabled when already bought or too expensive.
   - *Sell* (refund).
   The stats panel shows the decorated name (for example "Basic Tower Lv2 +Burning"), level, damage, range, fire rate, targeting,
   refund and the effect of each special upgrade. Burning critters get an orange ring, slowed critters a blue one.
 - **Resizing:** the interface is laid out on a fixed 1100 x 720 design area. The window can be resized freely (down to 640 x 440): the
   whole design area is scaled by one factor (the smaller of width / 1100 and height / 720) with a GDI world transform and centered,
   so the map, panel, buttons and texts grow and shrink together and nothing is stretched or cut off. Mouse positions are converted
   back to design coordinates (`toDesignPoint`), so clicks, hover and hit tests do not depend on the window size.
 - **Simulation:** time is advanced with a fixed 0.05 s step and an accumulator. Towers shoot first, then critters move and settle rewards and penalties, so the GUI stays consistent with the console driver.

\section limits Known limitations

 - The observer views are console (text) views. The Win32 graphical driver does not use them yet.
 - The graphical interface is only a thin layer. The only additions to the game classes are the read-only accessors needed for drawing: `Critter::getExactPosition`, `Tower::getUpgradeCost`, `Tower::getMaxLevel`, `Tower::getTargetingStrategy` and `TargetingStrategy::getName`.
 - Observers attached to a tower stay on the object they were attached to: after an upgrade wraps the tower, they are not moved to the new outer decorator (the GUI and `game_driver` do not attach observers).
 - Critters follow one fixed path, there is no reaction to towers.
 - Bloons TD features such as layered bloons or end-of-round cash are not used.
 - Game balance (costs, damage, rewards) has not been tuned beyond making the waves get harder.
 - The extra damage of a Level up is added to the main target only, even when the wrapped tower is a splash tower.
*/
